    {
      "id": "predict_the_change",
      "duration": "35s",
      "layout": {"type": "grid", "columns": 1, "rows": 1, "gap": "small"},
      "auto_visible": false,
      "show_progress_bar": false,
      "narration": "Imagine the same animal, but now the ears are much less floppy. What do you think should happen? The ear weight stays the same, because the model learned it earlier. But the ear clue is weaker, so its push gets smaller. That moves the combined lean back toward even, and the dog chance drops from sixty-four percent to about fifty-eight percent. That entire path, from a new input to a prediction, is called forward propagation.",
